-- allium: 3
-- skilj.allium

-- Scope: SkilJ, a Rust library for building event-sourced, DDD-style
--   applications backed by a single-instance Postgres database, exposing
--   commands and queries over GraphQL.
-- Includes: Bounded contexts, Commands, Events and their possible
--   origins, Command/Event type registration with JSON-Schema payload
--   definitions, the admin bounded context that registers other bounded
--   contexts and their types, the coarse shape of projections built from
--   events, raw event stream subscriptions with per-field filtering,
--   role-based access control over the GraphQL surface (Role/AccessToken,
--   scoped per bounded context to read/write/admin levels, plus a
--   superadmin flag on Role for creating new bounded contexts), and
--   per-field sensitivity marking on events with crypto-shredding for
--   erasure (SensitiveField, EncryptionKey, right-to-be-forgotten via
--   ForgetSubject).
-- Excludes:
--   - Enforcement of read-level access (querying projections, creating
--     subscriptions) and of sensitive-field decryption
--     (AccessToken.can_read_sensitive): both models exist, but there is
--     no query/subscription surface yet to gate either against - see the
--     detailed projection query surface exclusion below
--   - Retroactive encryption of historical events when a field is newly
--     marked sensitive: event sourcing is append-only, so this is a
--     structural consequence, not a gap (see EventType.sensitive_fields
--     and Event.encryption_keys)
--   - The encryption scheme itself (algorithm, key derivation, physical
--     key storage) - protect_sensitive_fields is a black box, consistent
--     with how Token.secret generation is already treated
--   - Multi-instance / distributed deployment
--   - The detailed projection query surface and GraphQL wire contract,
--     including how a Role's secret is transmitted (deferred to a later,
--     more detailed pass)
-- Dependencies: none yet

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value Filter {
    field: String              -- name of a field in the event type's schema
    operator: FilterOperator
    value: String               -- comparison value, serialised to match the field's declared type
}

value Metadata {
    type: String                -- denormalised name of the event/command type this message conforms to
    version: Integer             -- schema_version of the type at the time this message was created
    client_id: String          -- identifies the client/caller that produced this message in some cases it might refer to the original call, for an event it might be the client that issues the command
    created_at: Timestamp
}

value Tag {
    key: String                 -- shared across event types, e.g. "account"
    value: String                -- e.g. "123"
}

value TagMapping {
    key: String                 -- the tag key this mapping produces, e.g. "account" - the same key
                                 -- can be declared by multiple event types, so a consistency boundary
                                 -- can span all of them regardless of type (e.g. MoneyAdded and
                                 -- MoneySubtracted both mapping their account_id field to "account")
    field: String                -- name of the payload field this key's value is read from, for this
                                 -- event type specifically
}

value EventSpec {
    event_type: EventType
    payload: String              -- JSON-encoded, conforms to event_type.schema
}

value CommandDecision {
    accepted: Boolean
    events: List<EventSpec>      -- ignored when accepted = false
    rejection_reason: String?    -- domain-specific explanation from the decider; surfaced verbatim
                                  -- as the GraphQL error message when accepted = false. Producing a
                                  -- meaningful message here is the decider's responsibility - it
                                  -- needs domain knowledge this library doesn't have.
}

value SensitiveField {
    field: String                -- name of the payload field to store encrypted
    subject_key: String          -- canonical subject dimension this field belongs to
                                  -- (e.g. "user") - shared across event types, so the
                                  -- same EncryptionKey covers a subject regardless of
                                  -- which event type or payload field name captured it
    subject_field: String        -- name of the payload field this event type reads the
                                  -- concrete subject value from (e.g. "user_id")
}

value EncryptedPayload {
    payload: String                      -- payload with each declared sensitive field's
                                          -- value replaced by ciphertext
    encryption_keys: Set<EncryptionKey>  -- the EncryptionKeys used, one per distinct
                                          -- subject a sensitive field referenced
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum FilterOperator { equals | contains | is_like | greater_than | less_than }

enum AccessLevel { read | write | admin }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

entity BoundedContext {
    name: String
    status: active | archived              -- archived: no longer accepts new commands/events, but its
                                            -- history is retained (event sourcing is append-only; a
                                            -- bounded context is deactivated, never erased)

    event_types: EventType with bounded_context = this
    command_types: CommandType with bounded_context = this
    commands: Command with bounded_context = this
    events: Event with bounded_context = this
    projections: Projection with bounded_context = this
    subscriptions: Subscription with bounded_context = this
    access_tokens: AccessToken with bounded_context = this
    encryption_keys: EncryptionKey with bounded_context = this

    transitions status {
        active -> archived
        terminal: archived
    }
}

entity CommandType {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    name: String
    schema: String              -- JSON Schema describing the command's payload fields and optionality
    schema_version: Integer     -- increments on each schema change; changes must stay backwards compatible

    -- Mirrors EventType.tag_mappings: derives this command's
    -- consistency_tags from its own payload, using the same shared keys
    -- events are tagged with, so the two are directly comparable.
    tag_mappings: Set<TagMapping>
}

entity EventType {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    name: String
    schema: String              -- JSON Schema describing the event's payload fields and optionality
    schema_version: Integer     -- increments on each schema change; changes must stay backwards compatible

    -- Deliberately opt-in and small: each mapping promotes one payload
    -- field to an indexed, queryable tag. Only mapped fields are
    -- indexed - not every field - to keep the index footprint bounded.
    tag_mappings: Set<TagMapping>

    -- Opt-in, mirrors tag_mappings in shape. Adding an entry here only
    -- affects events created from this point forward - it does not
    -- retroactively encrypt this type's existing history, since events
    -- are immutable. See Event.encryption_keys to tell which historical
    -- events predate a given entry (and so still hold that field in
    -- plaintext).
    sensitive_fields: Set<SensitiveField>

    -- Command-triggered origin is always permitted; the other three
    -- origins require explicit opt-in per event type.
    external_creation_allowed: Boolean
    external_creation_tokens: ExternalEventToken with event_type = this
    direct_creation_allowed: Boolean
    direct_creation_tokens: DirectCreationToken with event_type = this
    system_triggered_allowed: Boolean
    system_triggered_schedule: String?     -- cron expression, evaluated by the core's own in-process
                                            -- scheduler; the core cannot rely on an OS-level cron
}

entity Token {
    id: String                             -- non-secret identifier, safe to log and store on events
    event_type: EventType
    secret: String                         -- opaque bearer credential (distinct from id)
    status: active | revoked
    created_at: Timestamp
    revoked_at: Timestamp? when status = revoked
    purpose: ExternalEventToken | DirectCreationToken

    -- Scoped to exactly one EventType: holding a token authorises acting
    -- (submitting an external event, or directly creating one) for that
    -- type only, nothing else. Lifecycle is tracked by the admin bounded
    -- context via TokenCreated/TokenRevoked.
    transitions status {
        active -> revoked
        terminal: revoked
    }
}

variant ExternalEventToken : Token {
    -- Held by an out-of-core adapter (e.g. a Kafka client, which needs OS
    -- facilities the core deliberately avoids) to authenticate against
    -- ExternalEventIngestion and fail independently if its own
    -- dependency is unavailable.
}

variant DirectCreationToken : Token {}

entity Role {
    id: String                             -- non-secret identifier, safe to log and store on events
    secret: String                         -- opaque bearer credential presented by GraphQL clients
    name: String
    superadmin: Boolean                    -- grants AddBoundedContext and all Role/AccessToken
                                            -- management, independently of any AccessToken below
    status: active | revoked
    created_at: Timestamp
    revoked_at: Timestamp? when status = revoked

    -- A Role reaches multiple bounded contexts by holding multiple
    -- AccessTokens, one per context - there is no single grant that
    -- spans contexts other than superadmin, which is orthogonal to
    -- context scoping entirely (see AccessToken).
    access_tokens: AccessToken with role = this

    transitions status {
        active -> revoked
        terminal: revoked
    }
}

entity AccessToken {
    role: Role
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    level: AccessLevel
    can_read_sensitive: Boolean             -- orthogonal to level: authorises decrypting
                                             -- sensitive fields within this token's
                                             -- bounded context. A write or admin token
                                             -- does not see decrypted sensitive values
                                             -- unless this is also true.
    status: active | revoked
    created_at: Timestamp
    revoked_at: Timestamp? when status = revoked

    -- Scoped to exactly one BoundedContext: holding this token authorises
    -- the given level within that context only, nothing else. Levels
    -- accumulate: read authorises querying and subscribing; write
    -- includes read and additionally authorises submitting commands;
    -- admin includes write and additionally authorises the
    -- admin-bounded-context mutations that target this specific context
    -- (archiving it, creating/revoking tokens for its own event types) -
    -- but not AddBoundedContext, which is gated by Role.superadmin
    -- instead, since creating a context isn't "for" any existing one.
    transitions status {
        active -> revoked
        terminal: revoked
    }
}

entity EncryptionKey {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    subject_key: String                    -- e.g. "user" - see SensitiveField.subject_key
    subject_value: String                  -- e.g. "123"
    status: active | destroyed
    created_at: Timestamp
    destroyed_at: Timestamp? when status = destroyed

    -- Crypto-shredding: destroying this key is what "forgets" a subject
    -- (see ForgetSubject). Historical events encrypted under it keep
    -- their ciphertext exactly as stored - event sourcing is
    -- append-only, so forgetting never edits or removes an event - the
    -- ciphertext simply becomes permanently unreadable. A later event
    -- for the same subject provisions a new active key; it does not
    -- revive this one.
    transitions status {
        active -> destroyed
        terminal: destroyed
    }
}

entity Command {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    command_type: CommandType
    payload: String                        -- JSON-encoded command data, conforms to command_type.schema
    metadata: Metadata                     -- client_id is the id of the Role behind the AccessToken
                                            -- that submitted this command (see CommandSubmission)

    -- Dynamic consistency boundary: the tags this command's decision was
    -- based on, and the highest Event.sequence observed among events
    -- matching those tags at decision time. Null when the command does
    -- not use a consistency boundary.
    consistency_tags: Set<Tag>
    consistency_boundary: Integer?

    -- Events produced by this command, stored in the same transaction.
    triggered_events: CommandTriggered with command = this
}

entity Event {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    event_type: EventType
    payload: String                        -- JSON-encoded event data, conforms to event_type.schema
    metadata: Metadata
    sequence: Integer                      -- append position within bounded_context: monotonically
                                            -- increasing, gapless, starting at 0
    tags: Set<Tag>                         -- derived from event_type.tag_mappings at creation time,
                                            -- not caller-supplied
    encryption_keys: Set<EncryptionKey>    -- captured at creation time: one key per distinct
                                            -- subject a sensitive field referenced at that
                                            -- moment. Empty if event_type declared no
                                            -- sensitive fields when this event was created -
                                            -- such an event holds those fields in plaintext,
                                            -- even if sensitive_fields later gains an entry
                                            -- for them (see EventType.sensitive_fields)
    origin: CommandTriggered | ExternalTriggered | DirectlyCreated | SystemTriggered
}

variant CommandTriggered : Event {
    command: Command
}

variant ExternalTriggered : Event {
    source_content: String                 -- source-specific payload, e.g. a raw JSON blob
    source_context: String?                -- source-specific provenance data; shape open, see open questions
    -- metadata.client_id holds the id of the ExternalEventToken used to submit this event
}

variant DirectlyCreated : Event {
    -- metadata.client_id holds the id of the DirectCreationToken used to create this event
}

variant SystemTriggered : Event {
    -- No variant-specific fields: the scheduler is the originator, so
    -- there is no external token or command to point back at. See
    -- SystemTriggeredClientIdIsSystem for the client_id constraint.
}

entity Projection {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    name: String

    -- No field-level structure yet (see open questions on the detailed
    -- projection query surface). Sensitivity is declared once, on the
    -- source EventType (see EventType.sensitive_fields) - a projection
    -- built from a sensitive field inherits the same restriction rather
    -- than re-declaring it.
}

entity Subscription {
    bounded_context: BoundedContext
    created_at: Timestamp
    kind: AllEventsSubscription | EventTypeSubscription
}

variant AllEventsSubscription : Subscription {}

variant EventTypeSubscription : Subscription {
    event_type: EventType
    filters: List<Filter>
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Defaults
------------------------------------------------------------

default BoundedContext admin = { name: "admin", status: active }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- next_sequence(bounded_context), used below in CreateExternalEvent,
-- CreateDirectEvent and ProcessCommand, and the consistency-boundary
-- re-check in ProcessCommand, are both enforced at the Postgres level
-- rather than in application code: a single row per bounded context
-- (e.g. in a sequence-counter table) is locked with SELECT ... FOR
-- UPDATE as part of the same transaction that inserts the new
-- Command/Event rows, then incremented and released on commit. This one
-- lock scope gives both guarantees for free - gapless assignment (the
-- counter only advances on a committed transaction, unlike a Postgres
-- SEQUENCE, which is non-transactional and does leave gaps on rollback)
-- and the DCB conflict re-check (no concurrent committer for the same
-- bounded context can be interleaved while the row is locked). Only the
-- final re-check and insert need the lock; reading matching events and
-- running decide() beforehand does not.
--
-- protect_sensitive_fields(event_type, payload), used in the same three
-- rules, follows a similar pattern: it get-or-creates the EncryptionKey
-- for each subject a sensitive field in event_type.sensitive_fields
-- references (each key provisioned exactly once, in the same
-- transaction as the event's own insert), then returns the payload with
-- those fields' values replaced by ciphertext plus the keys used. A
-- no-op - unchanged payload, empty key set - when sensitive_fields is
-- empty. Unlike next_sequence's target, EncryptionKey is a real,
-- rule-governed entity (see its transitions and ForgetSubject below);
-- this call only ever provisions - destruction is always a separate,
-- explicitly authorised action. It may be called more than once for the
-- same event_type/payload within one rule execution (see ProcessCommand)
-- and is idempotent when it is: repeat calls find the key already
-- provisioned rather than creating another one.

rule CreateExternalEvent {
    when: SubmitExternalEvent(adapter, event_type, payload, source_content, source_context?)
    requires: event_type.external_creation_allowed = true
    requires: adapter.event_type = event_type
    requires: adapter.status = active

    let protected = protect_sensitive_fields(event_type, payload)

    ensures:
        ExternalTriggered.created(
            bounded_context: event_type.bounded_context,
            event_type: event_type,
            payload: protected.payload,
            metadata: {
                type: event_type.name,
                version: event_type.schema_version,
                client_id: adapter.id,
                created_at: now
            },
            sequence: next_sequence(event_type.bounded_context),
            tags: derive_tags(event_type, payload),
            encryption_keys: protected.encryption_keys,
            source_content: source_content,
            source_context: source_context
        )
}

rule CreateDirectEvent {
    when: SubmitDirectEvent(adapter, event_type, payload)
    requires: event_type.direct_creation_allowed = true
    requires: adapter.event_type = event_type
    requires: adapter.status = active

    let protected = protect_sensitive_fields(event_type, payload)

    ensures:
        DirectlyCreated.created(
            bounded_context: event_type.bounded_context,
            event_type: event_type,
            payload: protected.payload,
            metadata: {
                type: event_type.name,
                version: event_type.schema_version,
                client_id: adapter.id,
                created_at: now
            },
            sequence: next_sequence(event_type.bounded_context),
            tags: derive_tags(event_type, payload),
            encryption_keys: protected.encryption_keys
        )
}

rule ProcessCommand {
    when: SubmitCommand(access_token, command_type, payload)
    requires: access_token.status = active
    requires: access_token.level in {write, admin}
    requires: access_token.bounded_context = command_type.bounded_context
    requires: command_type.bounded_context.status = active

    let consistency_tags = derive_tags(command_type, payload)
    let boundary = highest_sequence(command_type.bounded_context, consistency_tags)
    let matching_events =
        Events where bounded_context = command_type.bounded_context
            and sequence <= boundary
            and consistency_tags.any(t => t in tags)

    -- decide() is the plugged-in, bounded-context-specific decider (e.g.
    -- "is there enough balance to withdraw"). This library only owns the
    -- read/decide/commit shape, not the business logic itself. boundary
    -- and matching_events are evaluated fresh at commit time (see the
    -- Postgres locking note above the rules), so decide() always sees
    -- complete, current data for its tags - there is no separate staler
    -- state to reconcile against.
    let decision = decide(command_type, payload, matching_events)
    requires: decision.accepted = true

    ensures:
        let command = Command.created(
            bounded_context: command_type.bounded_context,
            command_type: command_type,
            payload: payload,
            metadata: {
                type: command_type.name,
                version: command_type.schema_version,
                client_id: access_token.role.id,
                created_at: now
            },
            consistency_tags: consistency_tags,
            consistency_boundary: boundary
        )
        for spec in decision.events:
            CommandTriggered.created(
                bounded_context: command_type.bounded_context,
                event_type: spec.event_type,
                payload: protect_sensitive_fields(spec.event_type, spec.payload).payload,
                metadata: {
                    type: spec.event_type.name,
                    version: spec.event_type.schema_version,
                    client_id: access_token.role.id,
                    created_at: now
                },
                sequence: next_sequence(command_type.bounded_context),
                tags: derive_tags(spec.event_type, spec.payload),
                encryption_keys: protect_sensitive_fields(spec.event_type, spec.payload).encryption_keys,
                command: command
            )

    @guidance
        -- Performance note, not a change in behaviour: decide() may be
        -- called an extra time in practice. An implementation can read
        -- matching_events and call decide() once optimistically, without
        -- holding the per-bounded-context lock, then take the lock only
        -- to fetch whatever events arrived after boundary (not a full
        -- re-scan) and call decide() again immediately with the
        -- complete set if anything new showed up. A conflict is a
        -- staleness signal, not an automatic rejection - only the final,
        -- fresh decision determines accepted/rejected. Either way the
        -- rule's own requires/ensures above is what must hold at commit.
}

-- The seven rules below are the admin bounded context's operations. In
-- principle each should itself be a Command producing a CommandTriggered
-- Event (BoundedContextAdded, BoundedContextArchived, EventTypeRegistered,
-- CommandTypeRegistered, TokenCreated, TokenRevoked) within the admin
-- bounded context, the same as any other bounded context's commands -
-- full dogfooding. That wiring depends on the still-open general
-- command-processing mechanism, so for now these are modelled as direct
-- effects, consistent with CreateExternalEvent and CreateDirectEvent
-- above.

rule AddBoundedContext {
    when: AddBoundedContext(caller, name)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    requires: not exists BoundedContext{name: name}
    ensures:
        BoundedContext.created(name: name, status: active)
}

rule ArchiveBoundedContext {
    when: ArchiveBoundedContext(access_token, bounded_context)
    requires: access_token.status = active
    requires: access_token.level = admin
    requires: access_token.bounded_context = bounded_context
    requires: bounded_context.status = active
    ensures:
        bounded_context.status = archived
}

-- RegisterEventType and RegisterCommandType below are idempotent upserts,
-- meant to be driven by a startup reconciliation loop: the running
-- instance discovers the EventType/CommandType definitions compiled into
-- its own binary (derived from the Rust type definitions themselves - a
-- black box here, the same as decide() and protect_sensitive_fields) and
-- registers each one against its bounded context. Re-registering an
-- unchanged shape is a no-op, so the loop can run on every startup.
--
-- schema_version tracks payload-shape compatibility specifically (see
-- EventType.schema_version), so it only advances when schema itself
-- changed. tag_mappings, sensitive_fields and the origin permissions are
-- always reconciled to the given values without a version bump - they
-- don't affect how a stored payload is interpreted.
--
-- Both are gated by an admin-level AccessToken for the target bounded
-- context, the same as the rules above. An AccessToken scoped to the
-- admin bounded context itself deliberately gains no new meaning here: a
-- grant on the admin context must not become a way to register types into
-- arbitrary other contexts.

rule RegisterEventType {
    when: RegisterEventType(access_token, bounded_context, name, schema, tag_mappings,
        sensitive_fields, external_creation_allowed, direct_creation_allowed,
        system_triggered_allowed, system_triggered_schedule?)
    requires: access_token.status = active
    requires: access_token.level = admin
    requires: access_token.bounded_context = bounded_context
    requires: bounded_context.status = active

    let existing = EventType{bounded_context: bounded_context, name: name}

    -- Tag mapping keys are additive-only on re-registration: events
    -- already carry tags under the keys declared so far, and
    -- EventTagsMatchDeclaredMappings checks a tag's key against the
    -- EventType's current mappings. Dropping a key would strand that
    -- history, so registration is rejected outright rather than silently
    -- losing it. The field a key reads from may change freely.
    requires:
        not exists existing
        or existing.tag_mappings.all(m => tag_mappings.any(n => n.key = m.key))

    let schema_changed = exists existing and existing.schema != schema

    ensures:
        if exists existing:
            existing.schema = schema
            existing.schema_version =
                if schema_changed: existing.schema_version + 1 else: existing.schema_version
            existing.tag_mappings = tag_mappings
            existing.sensitive_fields = sensitive_fields
            existing.external_creation_allowed = external_creation_allowed
            existing.direct_creation_allowed = direct_creation_allowed
            existing.system_triggered_allowed = system_triggered_allowed
            existing.system_triggered_schedule = system_triggered_schedule
        else:
            EventType.created(
                bounded_context: bounded_context,
                name: name,
                schema: schema,
                schema_version: 1,
                tag_mappings: tag_mappings,
                sensitive_fields: sensitive_fields,
                external_creation_allowed: external_creation_allowed,
                direct_creation_allowed: direct_creation_allowed,
                system_triggered_allowed: system_triggered_allowed,
                system_triggered_schedule: system_triggered_schedule
            )
}

rule RegisterCommandType {
    when: RegisterCommandType(access_token, bounded_context, name, schema, tag_mappings)
    requires: access_token.status = active
    requires: access_token.level = admin
    requires: access_token.bounded_context = bounded_context
    requires: bounded_context.status = active

    let existing = CommandType{bounded_context: bounded_context, name: name}

    -- Additive-only for the same reason as RegisterEventType, protecting
    -- CommandConsistencyTagsMatchDeclaredMappings: commands already
    -- recorded a consistency_tags set drawn from the keys declared so far.
    requires:
        not exists existing
        or existing.tag_mappings.all(m => tag_mappings.any(n => n.key = m.key))

    let schema_changed = exists existing and existing.schema != schema

    ensures:
        if exists existing:
            existing.schema = schema
            existing.schema_version =
                if schema_changed: existing.schema_version + 1 else: existing.schema_version
            existing.tag_mappings = tag_mappings
        else:
            CommandType.created(
                bounded_context: bounded_context,
                name: name,
                schema: schema,
                schema_version: 1,
                tag_mappings: tag_mappings
            )
}

rule CreateExternalEventToken {
    when: CreateExternalEventToken(access_token, event_type)
    requires: access_token.status = active
    requires: access_token.level = admin
    requires: access_token.bounded_context = event_type.bounded_context
    ensures:
        ExternalEventToken.created(
            id: generate_token_id(),
            event_type: event_type,
            secret: generate_token_secret(),
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule CreateDirectCreationToken {
    when: CreateDirectCreationToken(access_token, event_type)
    requires: access_token.status = active
    requires: access_token.level = admin
    requires: access_token.bounded_context = event_type.bounded_context
    ensures:
        DirectCreationToken.created(
            id: generate_token_id(),
            event_type: event_type,
            secret: generate_token_secret(),
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule RevokeToken {
    when: RevokeToken(access_token, token)
    requires: access_token.status = active
    requires: access_token.level = admin
    requires: access_token.bounded_context = token.event_type.bounded_context
    requires: token.status = active
    ensures:
        token.status = revoked
        token.revoked_at = now
}

-- The four rules below manage Role and AccessToken themselves - the
-- access-control layer that gates every rule above. All four require an
-- existing active superadmin Role, which resolves the obvious circularity
-- (access control needs access control) for every case except the very
-- first superadmin Role: see open questions. Modelled as direct effects
-- for the same reason as the five rules above.

rule CreateRole {
    when: CreateRole(caller, name, superadmin)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    ensures:
        Role.created(
            id: generate_token_id(),
            secret: generate_token_secret(),
            name: name,
            superadmin: superadmin,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule RevokeRole {
    when: RevokeRole(caller, role)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    requires: role.status = active
    ensures:
        role.status = revoked
        role.revoked_at = now
        for token in role.access_tokens where status = active:
            token.status = revoked
            token.revoked_at = now
}

rule GrantAccessToken {
    when: GrantAccessToken(caller, role, bounded_context, level, can_read_sensitive)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    requires: role.status = active
    requires: bounded_context.status = active
    requires: not exists AccessToken{role: role, bounded_context: bounded_context, status: active}
    ensures:
        AccessToken.created(
            role: role,
            bounded_context: bounded_context,
            level: level,
            can_read_sensitive: can_read_sensitive,
            status: active,
            created_at: now
        )
}

rule RevokeAccessToken {
    when: RevokeAccessToken(caller, access_token)
    requires: caller.status = active
    requires: caller.superadmin = true
    requires: access_token.status = active
    ensures:
        access_token.status = revoked
        access_token.revoked_at = now
}

-- ForgetSubject is not part of the superadmin-only group above: it is a
-- per-context admin operation, like ArchiveBoundedContext and the token
-- rules earlier - an admin-level AccessToken for a context can forget
-- subjects within it, the same way it can archive that context.

rule ForgetSubject {
    when: ForgetSubject(access_token, key)
    requires: access_token.status = active
    requires: access_token.level = admin
    requires: access_token.bounded_context = key.bounded_context
    requires: key.status = active
    ensures:
        key.status = destroyed
        key.destroyed_at = now
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant UniqueEventTypeNamePerContext {
    for a in EventTypes:
        for b in EventTypes:
            a != b and a.bounded_context = b.bounded_context implies a.name != b.name
}

invariant UniqueCommandTypeNamePerContext {
    for a in CommandTypes:
        for b in CommandTypes:
            a != b and a.bounded_context = b.bounded_context implies a.name != b.name
}

invariant DynamicConsistencyBoundaryHonoured {
    -- A command decides based on events matching its consistency_tags,
    -- up to consistency_boundary (the highest sequence observed at
    -- decision time). Any event with a matching tag and a higher
    -- sequence must be one of this command's own triggered events -
    -- never a foreign event that slipped in between the command's read
    -- and its commit.
    for c in Commands:
        for e in Events:
            (e.bounded_context = c.bounded_context and c.consistency_tags.any(t => t in e.tags)
                and e.sequence > c.consistency_boundary)
                implies e in c.triggered_events
}

invariant SequenceIsGaplessPerBoundedContext {
    for e in Events:
        e.sequence = 0 or Events.any(other => other.bounded_context = e.bounded_context and other.sequence = e.sequence - 1)
}

invariant ConsistencyTagKeysAreDeclared {
    -- A command may only consistency-check against tag keys that some
    -- EventType actually declares (and therefore indexes). This is the
    -- structural form of "can we verify an index exists for this tag at
    -- runtime".
    for c in Commands:
        for tag in c.consistency_tags:
            EventTypes.any(et => et.tag_mappings.any(m => m.key = tag.key))
}

invariant EventTagsMatchDeclaredMappings {
    for e in Events:
        for tag in e.tags:
            e.event_type.tag_mappings.any(m => m.key = tag.key)
}

invariant CommandConsistencyTagsMatchDeclaredMappings {
    for c in Commands:
        for tag in c.consistency_tags:
            c.command_type.tag_mappings.any(m => m.key = tag.key)
}

invariant SystemTriggeredClientIdIsSystem {
    -- The other three origins record a real originator in client_id (a
    -- token id, or the Role id behind the submitting AccessToken). A
    -- system-triggered event has none, so it records the literal
    -- "system" rather than borrowing an identity it doesn't have.
    for e in Events:
        e.origin = SystemTriggered implies e.metadata.client_id = "system"
}

invariant RevokedRoleImpliesTokensRevoked {
    for r in Roles:
        r.status = revoked implies r.access_tokens.all(t => t.status = revoked)
}

invariant UniqueActiveAccessPerRoleAndContext {
    -- A Role holds at most one active grant per bounded context, so
    -- "what can this Role do in this context" is never ambiguous.
    for a in AccessTokens:
        for b in AccessTokens:
            a != b and a.role = b.role and a.bounded_context = b.bounded_context
                implies not (a.status = active and b.status = active)
}

invariant UniqueActiveEncryptionKeyPerSubject {
    -- At most one active key per (bounded context, subject), so
    -- protect_sensitive_fields' get-or-create lookup is never ambiguous.
    for a in EncryptionKeys:
        for b in EncryptionKeys:
            a != b and a.bounded_context = b.bounded_context
                and a.subject_key = b.subject_key and a.subject_value = b.subject_value
                implies not (a.status = active and b.status = active)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor ReadAccess {
    -- Any active AccessToken authorises read: write and admin tokens
    -- include read implicitly, since they can already do strictly more.
    -- Not yet used in any surface - there is no query/subscription
    -- surface to attach it to (see open questions) - declared here so
    -- the level hierarchy is documented and ready when that surface is
    -- specified.
    identified_by: AccessToken where status = active
}

actor WriteAccess {
    identified_by: AccessToken where status = active and level in {write, admin}
}

actor AdminAccess {
    identified_by: AccessToken where status = active and level = admin
}

actor SensitiveDataAccess {
    -- Authorises reading the decrypted value of sensitive fields, on top
    -- of whatever level the underlying AccessToken already grants. Not
    -- yet used in any surface - same reason as ReadAccess: there is no
    -- query surface to attach it to yet.
    identified_by: AccessToken where status = active and can_read_sensitive = true
}

actor Superadmin {
    identified_by: Role where status = active and superadmin = true
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface ExternalEventIngestion {
    facing adapter: ExternalEventToken
    context event_type: EventType
        where this = adapter.event_type and external_creation_allowed = true and adapter.status = active

    provides:
        SubmitExternalEvent(adapter, event_type, payload, source_content, source_context?)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToEventType
        -- A token only authorises submitting events for the EventType it
        -- was issued for; it grants no other capability.

    @guidance
        -- Exposed as both a REST endpoint and a GraphQL mutation. The
        -- behavioural contract above is identical across both bindings;
        -- the choice of protocol is an implementation detail.
}

surface DirectEventCreation {
    facing adapter: DirectCreationToken
    context event_type: EventType
        where this = adapter.event_type and direct_creation_allowed = true and adapter.status = active

    provides:
        SubmitDirectEvent(adapter, event_type, payload)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToEventType
        -- A token only authorises creating events for the EventType it
        -- was issued for; it grants no other capability.

    @guidance
        -- Exposed as both a REST endpoint and a GraphQL mutation, the
        -- same as external event ingestion.
}

surface CommandSubmission {
    facing access_token: WriteAccess
    context command_type: CommandType
        where bounded_context = access_token.bounded_context and bounded_context.status = active

    provides:
        SubmitCommand(access_token, command_type, payload)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToBoundedContext
        -- A write-level token only authorises submitting commands for
        -- CommandTypes belonging to its own bounded context.

    @guidance
        -- Exposed as a GraphQL mutation, qualified by bounded context per
        -- the unified-graph naming scheme.
}

surface TypeRegistration {
    facing access_token: AdminAccess
    context bounded_context: BoundedContext
        where this = access_token.bounded_context and status = active

    provides:
        RegisterEventType(access_token, bounded_context, name, schema, tag_mappings,
            sensitive_fields, external_creation_allowed, direct_creation_allowed,
            system_triggered_allowed, system_triggered_schedule?)
        RegisterCommandType(access_token, bounded_context, name, schema, tag_mappings)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level token only authorises registering event and
        -- command types into its own bounded context, never another one.

    @guidance
        -- Typically driven by a startup reconciliation loop rather than by
        -- ad-hoc human calls, so the same shape is expected to be
        -- submitted repeatedly and unchanged. Still exposed as a GraphQL
        -- mutation as well, for manual and operational use.
}

surface EventTypeAdminOperations {
    facing access_token: AdminAccess
    context event_type: EventType
        where bounded_context = access_token.bounded_context

    provides:
        CreateExternalEventToken(access_token, event_type)
        CreateDirectCreationToken(access_token, event_type)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level token only authorises creating adapter tokens
        -- for EventTypes belonging to its own bounded context.
}

surface TokenRevocation {
    facing access_token: AdminAccess
    context token: Token
        where event_type.bounded_context = access_token.bounded_context and status = active

    provides:
        RevokeToken(access_token, token)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level token only authorises revoking adapter tokens
        -- whose EventType belongs to its own bounded context.
}

surface BoundedContextArchival {
    facing access_token: AdminAccess
    context bounded_context: BoundedContext
        where this = access_token.bounded_context and status = active

    provides:
        ArchiveBoundedContext(access_token, bounded_context)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level token only authorises archiving its own
        -- bounded context, never another one.
}

surface SubjectErasure {
    facing access_token: AdminAccess
    context key: EncryptionKey
        where bounded_context = access_token.bounded_context and status = active

    provides:
        ForgetSubject(access_token, key)

    @guarantee TokenScopedToBoundedContext
        -- An admin-level token only authorises forgetting subjects within
        -- its own bounded context.

    @guidance
        -- Exposed as a GraphQL mutation. There is no un-forget: destroying
        -- an EncryptionKey is irreversible by design.
}

surface BoundedContextCreation {
    facing caller: Superadmin

    provides:
        AddBoundedContext(caller, name)

    @guarantee SuperadminOnly
        -- Creating a bounded context isn't "for" any existing context,
        -- so it is gated by Role.superadmin rather than a per-context
        -- AccessToken.
}

surface AccessManagement {
    facing caller: Superadmin

    provides:
        CreateRole(caller, name, superadmin)
        RevokeRole(caller, role)
        GrantAccessToken(caller, role, bounded_context, level, can_read_sensitive)
        RevokeAccessToken(caller, access_token)

    @guarantee SuperadminOnly
        -- Only an active Role with superadmin = true may create or
        -- revoke Roles, or grant/revoke AccessTokens on their behalf -
        -- including its own. There is no self-service path.

    @guidance
        -- Exposed as GraphQL mutations. Bootstrapping the very first
        -- superadmin Role is unresolved - see open questions.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "External event context: how much source-specific metadata should be persisted, and does the shape vary per source type (e.g. Kafka vs webhook)?"

open question "Which filter operators are actually valid for a given field depends on that field's declared JSON Schema type (string vs integer vs identifier vs others). This isn't structurally enforced here - is that validation done at subscription-registration time, or at query time?"

open question "The all-events subscription per bounded context has 'limited to no filtering' - what, if anything, can it filter on (e.g. event type name only), versus the full per-field filtering on EventTypeSubscription?"

open question "Projections: how do they declare which event types they consume, how do they get rebuilt/kept up to date, and how are they exposed as GraphQL queries? Whichever design is chosen needs to carry sensitive fields through: a projection built from a sensitive EventType field should stay encrypted/redacted the same way the source event is, gated by the same AccessToken.can_read_sensitive."

open question "GraphQL surface: within one unified graph spanning all bounded contexts, commands become mutations and subscriptions/projections become queries/subscriptions, with names qualified by bounded context to stay unique. Write and admin mutations are now gated by AccessToken/Role (see Actor Declarations and the CommandSubmission/EventTypeAdminOperations/TokenRevocation/BoundedContextArchival/BoundedContextCreation/AccessManagement surfaces). Still open: how a Role's secret is transmitted on the wire, and how read-level access (AccessLevel.read already models it) gates the query/subscription surface once that surface itself is specified."

open question "Bootstrapping: CreateRole, RevokeRole, GrantAccessToken and RevokeAccessToken (see AccessManagement) all require an existing active superadmin Role, so nothing within the spec's own rules can create the very first one. Is this an out-of-band operational step (e.g. a seed value read once at first startup, outside the GraphQL surface entirely), or does the spec need to model an explicit bootstrap exception?"

open question "Schema versioning tracks a current schema_version counter per type, but not the historical schema text for prior versions. Do consumers reading old events need to look up the exact schema that was active for their recorded version, or is 'each version stays backwards compatible' sufficient without keeping that history?"

open question "decide() and CommandDecision are the plugged-in, bounded-context-specific decider - genuinely out of this library's scope, and now responsible for producing rejection_reason too. Is that plugin boundary worth specifying formally (e.g. as a Rust trait each bounded context implements), or left entirely to the consuming code with no contract here?"

open question "Tag keys declared via tag_mappings are plain strings with no namespace - could two unrelated bounded contexts collide on the same key (e.g. both using \"account\")? (Sequence scope is resolved: per bounded context, gapless. Serialisation mechanism is resolved: a Postgres row lock per bounded context, not application-level coordination.)"

open question "derive_tags(event_type, payload) is a black box: does TagMapping.field validate against event_type.schema at registration time (so a mapping can't reference a field that doesn't exist), or is that left unchecked since schema is just an opaque JSON Schema string to the spec? The same question applies to SensitiveField.field and SensitiveField.subject_field."

open question "Are there cases where a consistency boundary needs 'all events of a specific EventType' with no field-level tag at all, rather than a key/value tag? Raised as a possible exception but not confirmed as a real requirement."

open question "Historical data left behind when a field is newly marked sensitive: EventType.sensitive_fields only affects events created from that point forward (event sourcing is append-only, so existing events can't be edited in place - see Event.encryption_keys). Is 'it stays plaintext forever unless the whole bounded context is archived' acceptable, or does adding a sensitive_fields entry need a supported one-time backfill/purge path for the data that already exists, run outside the normal event-creation flow?"

open question "Should CommandType support sensitive_fields the same way EventType does? Command.payload is also stored permanently, but commands aren't queried the same way events/projections are, so it's unclear whether the same exposure risk applies."
